# SRG-APP-000880-WSR-000290  -  CAT II
# The web server must protect nonlocal maintenance sessions by separating
# the maintenance session from other network sessions with the system by
# logically separated communications paths.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations. Edit this
# file to change how this requirement is assessed here; it is read at run
# time. Regenerating overwrites it.

id:              SRG-APP-000880-WSR-000290
group:           V-264355
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      documented
revised:         yes
cci:             CCI-004192
control_rev5:    MA-4

contribution:    Splunk Enterprise can encrypt both of its own administrative entry points
                 - Splunk Web through web.conf [settings] enableSplunkWebSSL and the
                 splunkd management and KV Store ports through server.conf [sslConfig]
                 enableSplunkdSSL - which is the logical separation by encryption that the
                 requirement's own discussion names as the method; it can additionally
                 narrow admission to each of those listeners with acceptFrom, but the set
                 of networks that constitutes the maintenance path is an
                 organization-defined list and is not derivable from Splunk configuration.

governs:         server.conf - Core server settings, including the certificates and protocols used to
                 protect management and replication traffic.
governs:         web.conf - Configures the Splunk Web interface.

check:           web.conf [settings] enableSplunkWebSSL equals true
check:           server.conf [sslConfig] enableSplunkdSSL not_equals false
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          server.conf [sslConfig] enableSplunkdSSL https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/manage-users-and-security/10.4/secure-splunk-platform-communications-with-transport-layer-security-certificates/configure-tls-certificates-for-inter-splunk-communication
vendor:          web.conf [settings] enableSplunkWebSSL https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
# --- end vendor verification

external:        Whether the resulting management path is in fact separated from search and
                 data-ingestion sessions - a management VLAN or out-of-band network, a VPN
                 concentrator, a jump host, or a reverse proxy fronting Splunk Web - is
                 carried by the network baseline and evidenced in the system's network
                 documentation; no Splunk setting can report it.
external:        Selection of the networks that constitute the maintenance path is an
                 organization-defined list recorded in the system security plan and the
                 remote access policy. Where the organization elects to enforce that list
                 inside the application, web.conf [settings] acceptFrom
                 (web.conf.spec:1080) and server.conf [httpServer] acceptFrom
                 (server.conf.spec:1345) express it, but the list itself originates outside
                 the application and is not decidable from Splunk configuration.
external:        The physically separated branch of MA-4 (4) (b) (2), where the
                 organization elects physical rather than logical separation, is furnished
                 entirely by the network baseline; the application carries only the
                 logical, encryption-based branch.

citation:        CHANGED. Three corrections to the criterion as derived; the two TLS
                 conditions are kept, and both acceptFrom conditions are removed from the
                 pass criterion. 1. OVER-REACH - "web.conf [settings] acceptFrom present"
                 removed. The SRG check text (inventory/groups/MA.json,
                 SRG-APP-000880-WSR-000290) asks only that the maintenance session be
                 separated from other network sessions by logically separated
                 communications paths, and the discussion names exactly one mechanism:
                 "Communications paths can be logically separated using encryption."
                 Nothing in the check or fix text mentions source-network admission. There
                 is no product STIG rule for this requirement (stig_reference is empty and
                 the group's own determination records stig_reference_status
                 "no_reference"), so no product authority supplies an acceptFrom value
                 either. An instance that carries TLS on Splunk Web and on the splunkd
                 management port has done what the discussion names; failing it because
                 acceptFrom sits at its default converts the organization's decision to
                 separate the path at the network layer - the dependency this same
                 criterion already records externally - into an application finding. That
                 is case 3: the application's share is tested, the remainder is recorded,
                 and no finding is raised against Splunk. 2. DIRECTION / ineffective test -
                 "web.conf [settings] acceptFrom not_equals *" removed. Even if admission
                 control were in scope here, a plain string inequality cannot decide
                 whether the list is open. web.conf.spec:1080-1096 defines acceptFrom as an
                 ordered rule list ("Separate multiple rules with commas or spaces ... The
                 input applies rules in order, and uses the first one that matches") in
                 which a bare "*" is only one of four rule formats, and the specification's
                 own worked example at web.conf.spec:1093-1095 is "!10.1/16, *", described
                 as allowing "connections from everywhere except the 10.1.*.* network."
                 That value is as permissive as the "*" default for every network but one,
                 and it satisfies not_equals:*. The condition therefore returns a confident
                 PASS for precisely the configurations it exists to fail, which is worse
                 than having no condition. Writing it instead as a matches regex that
                 rejects any unprefixed "*" term would make it evaluable, but it would
                 still be the over-reach of item 1, so it is removed rather than repaired.
                 3. source_of_value corrected from srg_check_text to spec_default. The
                 check text for this requirement is tautological - it restates the title
                 and names no setting, value, or threshold - so it cannot be the source of
                 "true" or "false". The direction of each surviving condition is fixed by
                 the vendor-documented default: web.conf.spec:73 "Default: false" makes
                 enableSplunkWebSSL an affirmative requirement, and server.conf.spec:733
                 "Default: true" makes enableSplunkdSSL a prohibition on disabling.
                 Attributing those operands to the check text invites an assessor to look
                 for them there, find nothing, and reject the row. NOT CHANGED, and
                 deliberately so: the conf and stanza on enableSplunkdSSL. The derived
                 criterion placed it at server.conf [sslConfig], which is correct per
                 server.conf.spec:712 and :724 and matches the group's own recorded
                 mechanism. The defect is in the inventory, not the criterion -
                 assess/inventory/checks.tsv line 1180 assigns enableSplunkdSSL to web.conf
                 [settings] and flags it key_status "undeclared", which is accurate in that
                 web.conf has no such key: the only web.conf.spec occurrences of the string
                 are cross-references at :156 and :1626-equivalent prose. A condition bound
                 to that TSV row could never resolve against a merged configuration. The
                 row should be repointed to server.conf [sslConfig]; conditions must not be
                 attached to it as written. ALSO CONSIDERED AND EXCLUDED, because
                 checks.tsv declares them for this requirement and a reader will ask why
                 they are absent from conditions. requireClientCert (web.conf.spec:151) is
                 mutual authentication, not path separation; mandating it here would exceed
                 the discussion's stated method, and web.conf.spec:95-97 requires it to be
                 "false" when an encrypted private key is used, so a blanket demand would
                 contradict a supported TLS configuration. mgmtHostPort
                 (web.conf.spec:35-43) is "The IP address and host port where Splunk Web
                 looks for the splunkd process" - a client-side address with a documented
                 default of 0.0.0.0:8089 - so it does not restrict what the management
                 listener admits and a condition over it would test nothing about
                 separation. httpport (web.conf.spec:28) selects a listening port, and port
                 number alone carries no separation. listenOnIPv6 selects an address
                 family. server.conf [httpServerListener:<ip>:<port>] ssl
                 (server.conf.spec:1612-1629) does bind an additional REST listener to one
                 interface, but server.conf.spec:1620-1623 states these stanzas "need not
                 be present" on a normal instance and ssl defaults to "true", so a
                 condition over an optional stanza that usually does not exist would be
                 unsatisfiable on a correctly configured instance.
finding:         Splunk Enterprise does not encrypt both of its administrative entry points
                 - web.conf [settings] enableSplunkWebSSL does not have the value "true",
                 or server.conf [sslConfig] enableSplunkdSSL is set to "false" - so the
                 nonlocal maintenance session is not logically separated from other network
                 sessions by encryption to the extent the application is able to do so.
